      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Sacramento Northern Railway once ran through Canyon, linking the community to the wider Bay Area in the early 20th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon%2C_California",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1960s, a group of radicals created an enclave in Canyon, turning the valley into a counterculture hotspot amid the civil‑rights movement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.sfgate.com/travel/article/hidden-Bay-Area-town-Canyon-17470501.php",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Canyon’s first school opened in 1918, and the original building was replaced by a three‑classroom structure in 1993.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community’s history museum, run by the San Ramon Valley Historical Society, offers virtual programs that dig into Canyon’s archives.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
